From 0d342a7e001a2179df1f572f0c36f28c716e20f4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 17:21:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban: per-task incremental memo for the
 dispatcher's structural scans (G2c)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Why: the dispatcher's structural scans (`kanban_wake_check.foreign_ids`, `kanban_idle_candidates.candidate_task_ids`)
were memoized on the whole-board content revision. On the hub's default board that revision moves ~16x/min, so the
memo missed on nearly every tick, and each miss was a full scan: foreign_ids reads every `tasks` row (~5 ms, no index
on created_on) and runs ~7 times a tick via wake_due_continuations and recompute_ready (kanban_review_release.prepare);
candidate_task_ids ran 4x per apply_remote_archives plus a BEGIN + sqlite_master read_key each call. The live P1 at
854519ff measured the reclaim phase at 0.925% of a core against a 0.4% budget (G2b t_01a1d701bfd0abb6).

What: new `hermes_cli/kanban_task_memo.py`, a generic per-task memo driven by the P2 change cursor
(`kanban_task_change.changed_since`). State per (file identity, name, extra key) is an immutable (Cursor,
task_id -> value) pair, stored by compare-and-swap so it never goes backwards. Each call reads in one snapshot:
FULL -> full(conn); changed ids -> partial(conn, ids) merged (absent ids removed). Inside a caller transaction it
reads through and never stores; nothing is stored without a cursor. Consumers declare the columns they read: the
cursor is trusted only while each table's update trigger watches each column (a sync migration's ADD COLUMN
created_on on an open board is unwatched until a writer init reinstalls the triggers), and bookkeeping columns or
UNREAD_EVENT_KINDS (heartbeats) are refused. Truthy values only; memory bounded (MAX_SLOTS, MAX_ENTRIES).

foreign_ids and candidate_task_ids port to it with id-driven partial queries (O(changed) per dirty tick). An index
on tasks(created_on) was measured instead and rejected: fast only where the foreign count is 0 (hub, 0.015 ms), but
5.85 ms vs a 7.15 ms full scan on a worker view with 5,865 foreign rows; the memo is ~0.1 ms in both.
restore_missing_checks is unchanged: its task_wake_checks LEFT JOIN stays live (that table is unwatched), and a test
pins that a deleted probe is still re-registered with the memo warm. parent_states/unsatisfied_parents stay live.

Proof (hub, copy of the default board, deployed venv; base = 5eec3bedbbb, after = base + this change):
  t01a1d701_dirty.py N=15 median ms      before   after   card target
    apply_remote_archives                 9.02     0.33    <= 2
    wake_due_continuations                7.50     2.09    <= 3
    recompute_ready                       7.17     1.64    <= 3
    sweep_shared_blockers                40.32    35.10    (owned by G2c-blockers)
  scripts/kanban_reclaim_replay_bench.py --phases (new dirty-tick case) agrees: 8.51/7.29/6.86 -> 0.35/2.34/2.03.

Tests: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_task_memo.py (RED with the base consumers: 5 of 20 fail; GREEN here, 20 pass): created_on change,
insert and delete each reflected after one call; candidate event insert and status in/out; heartbeat-kind and
bookkeeping-column refusal; unwatched added column never trusted; txn write + rollback + revision reuse with
nothing stored inside the txn; current pair serves a read inside write_txn; FULL_MARK, file replace, shm change and
schema change read in full; two-thread race never moves the pair backwards; concurrent readers + writer; randomized
equivalence against a fresh full scan over 3x120 write sequences (incl. rollbacks and heartbeats); bounded memory;
a task_wake_checks delete still makes restore_missing_checks re-register. test_kanban_watcher_idle_work.py's memo
tests now count full vs partial scans.

Card: t_01a1db079d8d4505 (G2c-structural); design t01a1d563 §2 P2, review R13.
